{"event_index": 7, "stream": "stdout", "stream_sequence": 6, "received_utc": "2026-10-02T02:00:21.692815+00:00", "payload": {"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "\"C:\\\\Users\\\\lyh\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command '$n = 0; Get-Content -LiteralPath '\"'D:\\\\Documents\\\\Code\\\\Github\\\\clash-verge-ai-residential\\\\.agents\\\\skills\\\\trellis-start\\\\SKILL.md' | ForEach-Object { \"'$n++; '\"'{0}: {1}' -f \"'$n, $_ }'", "aggregated_output": "1: ---\r\n2: name: trellis-start\r\n3: description: \"Initializes an AI development session by reading workflow guides, developer identity, git status, active tasks, and project guidelines from .trellis/. Classifies incoming tasks and routes to brainstorm, direct edit, or task workflow. Use when beginning a new coding session, resuming work, starting a new task, or re-establishing project context.\"\r\n4: ---\r\n5: \r\n6: # Start Session\r\n7: \r\n8: Initialize a Trellis-managed development session. This platform has no session-start hook, so manually load the equivalent compact context by following these steps.\r\n9: \r\n10: ---\r\n11: \r\n12: ## Step 1: Current state\r\n13: Identity, git status, current task, active tasks, journal location.\r\n14: \r\n15: ```bash\r\n16: python ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py\r\n17: ```\r\n18: \r\n19: If this output includes a line beginning `Trellis update available:`, copy the full line verbatim when summarizing session context. Do not shorten operational command hints.\r\n20: \r\n21: ## Step 2: Workflow overview\r\n22: Compact Phase Index, request triage rules, planning artifact contract, and the step-detail command.\r\n23: \r\n24: ```bash\r\n25: python ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode phase\r\n26: ```\r\n27: \r\n28: Full guide in `.trellis/workflow.md` (read on demand).\r\n29: \r\n30: ## Step 3: Guideline indexes\r\n31: Discover packages + spec layers, then read each relevant index file.\r\n32: \r\n33: ```bash\r\n34: python ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode packages\r\n35: cat .trellis/spec/guides/index.md\r\n36: cat .trellis/spec/<package>/<layer>/index.md   # for each relevant layer\r\n37: ```\r\n38: \r\n39: Index files list the specific guideline docs to read when you actually start coding.\r\n40: \r\n41: ## Step 4: Decide next action\r\n42: From Step 1 you know the current task and status. Check the task directory:\r\n43: \r\n44: - **Active task status `planning` + no `prd.md`** �� Phase 1.1. Load the `trellis-brainstorm` skill.\r\n45: - **Active task status `planning` + `prd.md` exists** �� stay in Phase 1. Lightweight tasks can be PRD-only; complex tasks need `design.md` + `implement.md`. Load the relevant Phase 1 step detail before `task.py start`.\r\n46: - **Active task status `in_progress`** �� Phase 2 step 2.1. Load the step detail:\r\n47:   ```bash\r\n48:   python ./.trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode phase --step 2.1 --platform codex\r\n49:   ```\r\n50: - **No active task** �� classify first. For simple conversation / small task, ask only whether this turn should create a Trellis task. For complex work, ask whether you may create a Trellis task and enter planning. If the user says no, skip Trellis for this session.\r\n51: \r\n52: ---\r\n53: \r\n54: ## Skill routing (quick reference)\r\n55: \r\n56: | User intent | Skill |\r\n57: |---|---|\r\n58: | New feature / unclear requirements | `trellis-brainstorm` |\r\n59: | About to write code | `trellis-before-dev` |\r\n60: | Done coding / quality check | `trellis-check` |\r\n61: | Stuck / fixed same bug multiple times | `trellis-break-loop` |\r\n62: | Learned something worth capturing | `trellis-update-spec` |\r\n63: \r\n64: Full rules + anti-rationalization table in `.trellis/workflow.md`.\r\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}}
{"event_index": 8, "stream": "stdout", "stream_sequence": 7, "received_utc": "2026-10-02T02:00:21.760746+00:00", "payload": {"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "\"C:\\\\Users\\\\lyh\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command '$paths = @('\"'AGENTS.md', 'CONTEXT.md', 'docs/agents/harnesses.md', '.trellis/tasks/09-30-evergreen-runtime-validation/prd.md', '.trellis/spec/guides/index.md'); foreach (\"'$path in $paths) { Write-Output ('\"'FILE: ' + \"'$path); $n = 0; Get-Content -LiteralPath $path | ForEach-Object { $n++; '\"'{0}: {1}' -f \"'$n, $_ } }'", "aggregated_output": "FILE: AGENTS.md\r\n1: <!-- TRELLIS:START -->\r\n2: # Trellis Instructions\r\n3: \r\n4: These instructions are for AI assistants working in this project.\r\n5: \r\n6: This project is managed by Trellis. The working knowledge you need lives under `.trellis/`:\r\n7: \r\n8: - `.trellis/workflow.md` �� development phases, when to create tasks, skill routing\r\n9: - `.trellis/spec/` �� package- and layer-scoped coding guidelines (read before writing code in a given layer)\r\n10: - `.trellis/workspace/` �� per-developer journals and session traces\r\n11: - `.trellis/tasks/` �� active and archived tasks (PRDs, research, jsonl context)\r\n12: \r\n13: If a Trellis command is available on your platform (e.g. `/trellis:finish-work`, `/trellis:continue`), prefer it over manual steps. Not every platform exposes every command.\r\n14: \r\n15: If you're using Codex or another agent-capable tool, additional project-scoped helpers may live in:\r\n16: - `.agents/skills/` �� reusable Trellis skills\r\n17: - `.codex/agents/` �� optional custom subagents\r\n18: \r\n19: Managed by Trellis. Edits outside this block are preserved; edits inside may be overwritten by a future `trellis update`.\r\n20: \r\n21: <!-- TRELLIS:END -->\r\n22: \r\n23: # Project contract\r\n24: \r\n25: This `AGENTS.md` is the shared project contract for Claude Code, Codex, Grok\r\n26: Build, Kimi Code, and OMP. Shared facts live in this file. Do not depend on\r\n27: `@` imports to expand them. Claude-specific loader notes belong in `CLAUDE.md`\r\n28: only. `AGENTS.md` must not import `CLAUDE.md`.\r\n29: \r\n30: ## Authorization and review\r\n31: \r\n32: These rules apply to Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, Kimi Code, and OMP.\r\n33: Read-only review may run checks and write explicitly authorized research\r\n34: artifacts. It must not repair product code or configuration. Self-fix in any\r\n35: `trellis-check` role or skill applies only after implementation is approved,\r\n36: within the approved task and file scope. A task directory, an `in_progress`\r\n37: status, or an available write tool does not grant authorization. If a finding\r\n38: needs additional files, behavior, permissions, or a lower validation gate,\r\n39: return the finding to the planning/review model before expanding the work.\r\n40: \r\n41: Harness capabilities and role prompts do not expand authorization. Do not\r\n42: auto-trust projects or hooks, bypass permissions, or treat prompt recursion\r\n43: guards as a hard sandbox. Dispatch only through the applicable workflow;\r\n44: already dispatched implement/check agents must not spawn those roles again.\r\n45: \r\n46: ## What this repository contains\r\n47: \r\n48: This repository is three surfaces, not a single no-dependency script:\r\n49: \r\n50: - Clash Verge Rev **global extension script** `clash-verge-ai-residential.js`\r\n51:   (repo root, vanilla CommonJS, Node.js ��18, zero third-party dependencies).\r\n52:   Users paste that file into Clash Verge to route core AI traffic through a\r\n53:   residential SOCKS5 chain. Other traffic stays on the airport proxy.\r\n54: - **ResiWatch** in `residential-monitor/` (React frontend, Tauri/Rust backend,\r\n55:   SQLite ledger). Windows 11 NSIS current-user desktop app. It does not read\r\n56:   `*.local.toml` and does not change the pasteable extension.\r\n57: - **VitePress docs** in `docs/` (Node.js 22+). Default locale is Chinese at\r\n58:   `docs/*.md`. English sources are `docs/en/`.\r\n59: \r\n60: Domain terms: `CONTEXT.md`.\r\n61: \r\n62: ## Toolchains by path\r\n63: \r\n64: Root / extension / `scripts/` / `tests/` �� Node.js 18+, zero third-party\r\n65: dependencies, `node:test` (no third-party test framework):\r\n66: \r\n67: - `npm run check`\r\n68: - `npm test`\r\n69: - `npm run ci`\r\n70: - `npm run check:secrets`\r\n71: \r\n72: `residential-monitor/` �� React frontend plus Tauri/Rust:\r\n73: \r\n74: - `just monitor-check`\r\n75: - `just ci` (`monitor-check`, then root `npm run ci`)\r\n76: \r\n77: `docs/` �� Node.js 22+, dependencies in `docs/package.json`:\r\n78: \r\n79: - `just docs-build`\r\n80: - `npm --prefix docs run build`\r\n81: \r\n82: Docs is not part of `just ci`.\r\n83: \r\n84: ## Verification\r\n85: \r\n86: Local full product gate: `just ci` (monitor + root). `just ci` is not equal to\r\n87: `npm run ci`. `just ci` does not build docs.\r\n88: \r\n89: Local docs: `just docs-build` (independent of `just ci`).\r\n90: \r\n91: Dependency security: `just dependency-audit` is independent of `just ci` and\r\n92: `just docs-build`. Run it for dependency/lockfile and audit-gate changes. It\r\n93: runs full monitor/docs npm audits, including dev dependencies, with\r\n94: `--include=dev --audit-level=high`, then\r\n95: `cargo audit --file residential-monitor/src-tauri/Cargo.lock`. The explicit\r\n96: `--include=dev` keeps development dependencies in the audit when environment\r\n97: or npm configuration sets `omit=dev`.\r\n98: Prerequisites: npm, Cargo, an already installed `cargo-audit`, and network\r\n99: access to the configured npm registry and RustSec advisory database. Do not\r\n100: install tools globally without authorization. Registry/database/network or\r\n101: missing-tool failures are blocked checks, not vulnerability findings or PASS.\r\n102: Keep RustSec unmaintained/unsound warnings separate from vulnerabilities and\r\n103: record the affected target. A successful `npm ci` does not satisfy the audit\r\n104: gate. These rules apply to Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, Kimi Code, and OMP.\r\n105: \r\n106: Hosted GitHub CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`):\r\n107: \r\n108: - Ubuntu Node.js 18/20/22 and Windows Node.js 22 run `npm run ci`.\r\n109: - Windows `monitor` job: version alignment, then seven separate pwsh native\r\n110:   steps (frontend install, full npm audit, frontend check, Rust fmt, clippy,\r\n111:   workspace tests, secret scan).\r\n112: - Ubuntu Node.js 22 `docs` job: `npm --prefix docs ci`, independent\r\n113:   `npm --prefix docs audit --include=dev --audit-level=high`, then\r\n114:   `npm --prefix docs run build`.\r\n115: - Aggregate job named `Required checks` needs `[test, monitor, docs]`.\r\n116: - RustSec audit remains an independent local check; hosted CI does not run it.\r\n117: \r\n118: Root-only `npm run ci` is syntax check plus listed Node tests plus the secret\r\n119: scan. Use `just ci` when monitor must pass as well.\r\n120: \r\n121: For host integration, DNS, or routing changes, also test a sanitized real Clash\r\n122: profile when practical. Node tests do not emulate the Clash JavaScript host or\r\n123: Mihomo.\r\n124: \r\n125: ## Boundaries\r\n126: \r\n127: - Never put real credentials in the public template. `HOME_PROXY_TEMPLATE`\r\n128:   `server` / `username` / `password` stay `\"xxx\"` / `\"\"`. `npm run check:secrets`\r\n129:   enforces this.\r\n130: - Never commit `*.local.toml` or `*.local.js`. By default, agents must not\r\n131:   hand-edit either file. For personal tuning, produce recommendations for\r\n132:   the user to apply to `*.local.toml`. An agent may edit that TOML only when\r\n133:   the user explicitly authorizes the exception and names the change scope;\r\n134:   a skill or task does not supply that exception. Never hand-edit generated\r\n135:   `*.local.js`. Generate local output with `just render-local`. First run\r\n136:   copies `*.local.toml.example` to `*.local.toml` and exits 1 until the user\r\n137:   fills in the file.\r\n138: - New routed domains need an official source or sanitized Connections evidence\r\n139:   plus a negative test. Broad provider suffixes, marketplace/CDN, and telemetry\r\n140:   are rejected by default (README and PR template).\r\n141: - Production controller, credential, and routing-algorithm changes need an\r\n142:   active task that names those files. Do not mix those edits into contract,\r\n143:   docs-toolchain, or harness work.\r\n144: \r\n145: ## Spec navigation\r\n146: \r\n147: - `.trellis/spec/frontend/` �� pasteable extension and local renderer at repo\r\n148:   root. That package is not the ResiWatch React app.\r\n149: - `.trellis/spec/residential-monitor/` �� package index. It points at\r\n150:   `backend/`, `frontend/`, and `storage/` layer indexes.\r\n151: - `python .trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode packages` lists first-layer\r\n152:   specs.\r\n153: \r\n154: Read the layer that matches the files you change. Do not apply the root\r\n155: frontend spec to `residential-monitor/src/`.\r\n156: \r\n157: ## Language split\r\n158: \r\n159: - Code comments, error messages, `CHANGELOG.md`, and `docs/` (except `docs/en/`) are Chinese.\r\n160: - `docs/en/` is the English docs-site tree.\r\n161: - `package.json`, CI, and PR/issue templates are English.\r\n162: - This Trellis spec tree is English.\r\n163: \r\n164: Match the file you edit. Root extension style: 2-space indent, double quotes,\r\n165: `\"use strict\"`, CommonJS. No root linter/formatter. Keep syntax clean with\r\n166: `npm run check`. That style does not apply to `residential-monitor/` or `docs/`.\r\n167: \r\n168: Commits: `<type>: [AI] <gitmoji> <Chinese subject>` (example:\r\n169: `feat: [AI] ? ���ӱ���������Ⱦ`).\r\n170: \r\n171: Business skill source: `skills/residential-rule-tuning/`. Install with\r\n172: `just install-skills`. 