{"event_index": 28, "stream": "stdout", "stream_sequence": 27, "received_utc": "2026-10-02T02:00:56.605865+00:00", "payload": {"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "\"C:\\\\Users\\\\lyh\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command '$n = 0; Get-Content -Encoding UTF8 -LiteralPath '\"'AGENTS.md' | ForEach-Object { \"'$n++; $line = '\"'{0}: {1}' -f \"'$n, $_; [regex]::Replace($line, '\"'[\"'^'\"\\\\x00-\\\\x7F]', { param(\"'$m) '\"'\\\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]\"'$m.Value }) }'", "aggregated_output": "1: <!-- TRELLIS:START -->\r\n2: # Trellis Instructions\r\n3: \r\n4: These instructions are for AI assistants working in this project.\r\n5: \r\n6: This project is managed by Trellis. The working knowledge you need lives under `.trellis/`:\r\n7: \r\n8: - `.trellis/workflow.md`  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  development phases, when to create tasks, skill routing\r\n9: - `.trellis/spec/`  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  package- and layer-scoped coding guidelines (read before writing code in a given layer)\r\n10: - `.trellis/workspace/`  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  per-developer journals and session traces\r\n11: - `.trellis/tasks/`  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  active and archived tasks (PRDs, research, jsonl context)\r\n12: \r\n13: If a Trellis command is available on your platform (e.g. `/trellis:finish-work`, `/trellis:continue`), prefer it over manual steps. Not every platform exposes every command.\r\n14: \r\n15: If you're using Codex or another agent-capable tool, additional project-scoped helpers may live in:\r\n16: - `.agents/skills/`  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  reusable Trellis skills\r\n17: - `.codex/agents/`  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  optional custom subagents\r\n18: \r\n19: Managed by Trellis. Edits outside this block are preserved; edits inside may be overwritten by a future `trellis update`.\r\n20: \r\n21: <!-- TRELLIS:END -->\r\n22: \r\n23: # Project contract\r\n24: \r\n25: This `AGENTS.md` is the shared project contract for Claude Code, Codex, Grok\r\n26: Build, Kimi Code, and OMP. Shared facts live in this file. Do not depend on\r\n27: `@` imports to expand them. Claude-specific loader notes belong in `CLAUDE.md`\r\n28: only. `AGENTS.md` must not import `CLAUDE.md`.\r\n29: \r\n30: ## Authorization and review\r\n31: \r\n32: These rules apply to Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, Kimi Code, and OMP.\r\n33: Read-only review may run checks and write explicitly authorized research\r\n34: artifacts. It must not repair product code or configuration. Self-fix in any\r\n35: `trellis-check` role or skill applies only after implementation is approved,\r\n36: within the approved task and file scope. A task directory, an `in_progress`\r\n37: status, or an available write tool does not grant authorization. If a finding\r\n38: needs additional files, behavior, permissions, or a lower validation gate,\r\n39: return the finding to the planning/review model before expanding the work.\r\n40: \r\n41: Harness capabilities and role prompts do not expand authorization. Do not\r\n42: auto-trust projects or hooks, bypass permissions, or treat prompt recursion\r\n43: guards as a hard sandbox. Dispatch only through the applicable workflow;\r\n44: already dispatched implement/check agents must not spawn those roles again.\r\n45: \r\n46: ## What this repository contains\r\n47: \r\n48: This repository is three surfaces, not a single no-dependency script:\r\n49: \r\n50: - Clash Verge Rev **global extension script** `clash-verge-ai-residential.js`\r\n51:   (repo root, vanilla CommonJS, Node.js  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value 18, zero third-party dependencies).\r\n52:   Users paste that file into Clash Verge to route core AI traffic through a\r\n53:   residential SOCKS5 chain. Other traffic stays on the airport proxy.\r\n54: - **ResiWatch** in `residential-monitor/` (React frontend, Tauri/Rust backend,\r\n55:   SQLite ledger). Windows 11 NSIS current-user desktop app. It does not read\r\n56:   `*.local.toml` and does not change the pasteable extension.\r\n57: - **VitePress docs** in `docs/` (Node.js 22+). Default locale is Chinese at\r\n58:   `docs/*.md`. English sources are `docs/en/`.\r\n59: \r\n60: Domain terms: `CONTEXT.md`.\r\n61: \r\n62: ## Toolchains by path\r\n63: \r\n64: Root / extension / `scripts/` / `tests/`  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  Node.js 18+, zero third-party\r\n65: dependencies, `node:test` (no third-party test framework):\r\n66: \r\n67: - `npm run check`\r\n68: - `npm test`\r\n69: - `npm run ci`\r\n70: - `npm run check:secrets`\r\n71: \r\n72: `residential-monitor/`  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  React frontend plus Tauri/Rust:\r\n73: \r\n74: - `just monitor-check`\r\n75: - `just ci` (`monitor-check`, then root `npm run ci`)\r\n76: \r\n77: `docs/`  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  Node.js 22+, dependencies in `docs/package.json`:\r\n78: \r\n79: - `just docs-build`\r\n80: - `npm --prefix docs run build`\r\n81: \r\n82: Docs is not part of `just ci`.\r\n83: \r\n84: ## Verification\r\n85: \r\n86: Local full product gate: `just ci` (monitor + root). `just ci` is not equal to\r\n87: `npm run ci`. `just ci` does not build docs.\r\n88: \r\n89: Local docs: `just docs-build` (independent of `just ci`).\r\n90: \r\n91: Dependency security: `just dependency-audit` is independent of `just ci` and\r\n92: `just docs-build`. Run it for dependency/lockfile and audit-gate changes. It\r\n93: runs full monitor/docs npm audits, including dev dependencies, with\r\n94: `--include=dev --audit-level=high`, then\r\n95: `cargo audit --file residential-monitor/src-tauri/Cargo.lock`. The explicit\r\n96: `--include=dev` keeps development dependencies in the audit when environment\r\n97: or npm configuration sets `omit=dev`.\r\n98: Prerequisites: npm, Cargo, an already installed `cargo-audit`, and network\r\n99: access to the configured npm registry and RustSec advisory database. Do not\r\n100: install tools globally without authorization. Registry/database/network or\r\n101: missing-tool failures are blocked checks, not vulnerability findings or PASS.\r\n102: Keep RustSec unmaintained/unsound warnings separate from vulnerabilities and\r\n103: record the affected target. A successful `npm ci` does not satisfy the audit\r\n104: gate. These rules apply to Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, Kimi Code, and OMP.\r\n105: \r\n106: Hosted GitHub CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`):\r\n107: \r\n108: - Ubuntu Node.js 18/20/22 and Windows Node.js 22 run `npm run ci`.\r\n109: - Windows `monitor` job: version alignment, then seven separate pwsh native\r\n110:   steps (frontend install, full npm audit, frontend check, Rust fmt, clippy,\r\n111:   workspace tests, secret scan).\r\n112: - Ubuntu Node.js 22 `docs` job: `npm --prefix docs ci`, independent\r\n113:   `npm --prefix docs audit --include=dev --audit-level=high`, then\r\n114:   `npm --prefix docs run build`.\r\n115: - Aggregate job named `Required checks` needs `[test, monitor, docs]`.\r\n116: - RustSec audit remains an independent local check; hosted CI does not run it.\r\n117: \r\n118: Root-only `npm run ci` is syntax check plus listed Node tests plus the secret\r\n119: scan. Use `just ci` when monitor must pass as well.\r\n120: \r\n121: For host integration, DNS, or routing changes, also test a sanitized real Clash\r\n122: profile when practical. Node tests do not emulate the Clash JavaScript host or\r\n123: Mihomo.\r\n124: \r\n125: ## Boundaries\r\n126: \r\n127: - Never put real credentials in the public template. `HOME_PROXY_TEMPLATE`\r\n128:   `server` / `username` / `password` stay `\"xxx\"` / `\"\"`. `npm run check:secrets`\r\n129:   enforces this.\r\n130: - Never commit `*.local.toml` or `*.local.js`. By default, agents must not\r\n131:   hand-edit either file. For personal tuning, produce recommendations for\r\n132:   the user to apply to `*.local.toml`. An agent may edit that TOML only when\r\n133:   the user explicitly authorizes the exception and names the change scope;\r\n134:   a skill or task does not supply that exception. Never hand-edit generated\r\n135:   `*.local.js`. Generate local output with `just render-local`. First run\r\n136:   copies `*.local.toml.example` to `*.local.toml` and exits 1 until the user\r\n137:   fills in the file.\r\n138: - New routed domains need an official source or sanitized Connections evidence\r\n139:   plus a negative test. Broad provider suffixes, marketplace/CDN, and telemetry\r\n140:   are rejected by default (README and PR template).\r\n141: - Production controller, credential, and routing-algorithm changes need an\r\n142:   active task that names those files. Do not mix those edits into contract,\r\n143:   docs-toolchain, or harness work.\r\n144: \r\n145: ## Spec navigation\r\n146: \r\n147: - `.trellis/spec/frontend/`  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  pasteable extension and local renderer at repo\r\n148:   root. That package is not the ResiWatch React app.\r\n149: - `.trellis/spec/residential-monitor/`  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  package index. It points at\r\n150:   `backend/`, `frontend/`, and `storage/` layer indexes.\r\n151: - `python .trellis/scripts/get_context.py --mode packages` lists first-layer\r\n152:   specs.\r\n153: \r\n154: Read the layer that matches the files you change. Do not apply the root\r\n155: frontend spec to `residential-monitor/src/`.\r\n156: \r\n157: ## Language split\r\n158: \r\n159: - Code comments, error messages, `CHANGELOG.md`, and `docs/` (except `docs/en/`) are Chinese.\r\n160: - `docs/en/` is the English docs-site tree.\r\n161: - `package.json`, CI, and PR/issue templates are English.\r\n162: - This Trellis spec tree is English.\r\n163: \r\n164: Match the file you edit. Root extension style: 2-space indent, double quotes,\r\n165: `\"use strict\"`, CommonJS. No root linter/formatter. Keep syntax clean with\r\n166: `npm run check`. That style does not apply to `residential-monitor/` or `docs/`.\r\n167: \r\n168: Commits: `<type>: [AI] <gitmoji> <Chinese subject>` (example:\r\n169: `feat: [AI]  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value   param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value `).\r\n170: \r\n171: Business skill source: `skills/residential-rule-tuning/`. Install with\r\n172: `just install-skills`. See `docs/agents/`.\r\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}}
{"event_index": 29, "stream": "stdout", "stream_sequence": 28, "received_utc": "2026-10-02T02:00:56.640117+00:00", "payload": {"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "\"C:\\\\Users\\\\lyh\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command '$n = 0; Get-Content -Encoding UTF8 -LiteralPath '\"'.trellis/spec/guides/index.md' | ForEach-Object { \"'$n++; $line = '\"'{0}: {1}' -f \"'$n, $_; [regex]::Replace($line, '\"'[\"'^'\"\\\\x00-\\\\x7F]', { param(\"'$m) '\"'\\\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]\"'$m.Value }) }'", "aggregated_output": "1: # Thinking Guides\r\n2: \r\n3: > **Purpose**: Expand your thinking to catch things you might not have considered.\r\n4: \r\n5: ---\r\n6: \r\n7: ## Why Thinking Guides?\r\n8: \r\n9: **Most bugs and tech debt come from \"didn't think of that\"**, not from lack of skill:\r\n10: \r\n11: - Didn't think about what happens at layer boundaries  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  cross-layer bugs\r\n12: - Didn't think about code patterns repeating  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  duplicated code everywhere\r\n13: - Didn't think about edge cases  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  runtime errors\r\n14: - Didn't think about future maintainers  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  unreadable code\r\n15: \r\n16: These guides help you **ask the right questions before coding**.\r\n17: \r\n18: ---\r\n19: \r\n20: ## Available Guides\r\n21: \r\n22: | Guide | Purpose | When to Use |\r\n23: |-------|---------|-------------|\r\n24: | [Code Reuse Thinking Guide](./code-reuse-thinking-guide.md) | Identify patterns and reduce duplication | When you notice repeated patterns |\r\n25: | [Cross-Layer Thinking Guide](./cross-layer-thinking-guide.md) | Think through data flow across layers | Features spanning multiple layers |\r\n26: \r\n27: ---\r\n28: \r\n29: ## Quick Reference: Thinking Triggers\r\n30: \r\n31: ### When to Think About Cross-Layer Issues\r\n32: \r\n33: - [ ] Feature touches 3+ layers (API, Service, Component, Database)\r\n34: - [ ] Data format changes between layers\r\n35: - [ ] Multiple consumers need the same data\r\n36: - [ ] You're not sure where to put some logic\r\n37: - [ ] You are adding an event kind, JSONL record, RPC payload, or config field\r\n38: - [ ] UI / command code starts casting raw payload fields directly\r\n39: - [ ] Real-time summary or Top 1 is derived from a truncated `rows` page instead of the query snapshot `summary`\r\n40: - [ ] You add an `AppFacade` write path (report, targets, alerts, backup) without a Recovery-only `recovery_only` test\r\n41: \r\n42:  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  Read [Cross-Layer Thinking Guide](./cross-layer-thinking-guide.md)\r\n43: \r\n44: ### When Changing AI Residential Domains\r\n45: \r\n46: - [ ] Official firewall/allowlist pages say \"do not block\". They are not a contract to inject every listed host into `AI- param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value `.\r\n47: - [ ] Clash `DOMAIN` / `DOMAIN-SUFFIX` match host/SNI only. A path such as `/backend-api/codex/responses` cannot be split from other paths on the same host.\r\n48: - [ ] Values removed from activation must stay in `allPossible*` so old rules and DNS keys are cleaned.\r\n49: - [ ] Do not retire `daily-cloudcode-pa.googleapis.com`. Antigravity `language_server` uses it as `--cloud_code_endpoint`. A missing official firewall line is not evidence that the host is unused.\r\n50: - [ ] Routing rules and residential `nameserver-policy` can be decoupled. Extra sites use `EXTRA_SITES`; `residentialDns: false` means inject `DOMAIN-SUFFIX` / `DOMAIN` only.\r\n51: - [ ] New domains need a real TLS handshake from the intended DNS vantage, not only a host string. CDN/GeoDNS (for example ESA) can pick a broken edge on residential DoH while airport-upstream DoH works.\r\n52: - [ ] `routing.extra` is a category gate. Site switches are separate flat keys such as `routing.extra_anyrouter`.\r\n53: \r\n54:  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  Product contract: [docs/routing-scope.md](../../../docs/routing-scope.md)\r\n55: \r\n56: ### When to Think About Code Reuse\r\n57: \r\n58: - [ ] You're writing similar code to something that exists\r\n59: - [ ] You see the same pattern repeated 3+ times\r\n60: - [ ] You're adding a new field to multiple places\r\n61: - [ ] **You're modifying any constant or config**\r\n62: - [ ] **You're creating a new utility/helper function**  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  Search first!\r\n63: - [ ] Two files read the same untyped payload field with local casts\r\n64: - [ ] Multiple branches update the same derived state from `kind` / `action`\r\n65: \r\n66:  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  Read [Code Reuse Thinking Guide](./code-reuse-thinking-guide.md)\r\n67: \r\n68: ### When Verifying AI Cross-Review Results\r\n69: \r\n70: - [ ] Reviewer claims \"user input can be malicious\"  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  Check the actual data source (internal manifest? user config? external API?)\r\n71: - [ ] Reviewer flags \"missing validation\"  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  Is the data from a trusted internal source?\r\n72: - [ ] Reviewer says \"behavior change\"  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  Read the code comments  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  is it intentional design?\r\n73: - [ ] Reviewer identifies a \"bug\" in test  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  Mentally delete the feature being tested  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  does the test still pass? If yes  param($m) '\\u{0:x4}' -f [int][char]$m.Value  tautological test\r\n74: \r\n75: **Common AI reviewer false-positive patterns**:\r\n76: 1. **Trust boundary confusion**: Treating internal data (bundled JSON manifests) as untrusted external input\r\n77: 2. **Ignoring design comments**: Flagging intentional behavior documented in code comments as bugs\r\n78: 3. **Variable misreading**: Not tracing a variable to its actual definition (e.g., Map keyed by path vs name)\r\n79: \r\n80: **Verification rule**: Every CRITICAL/WARNING finding must be verified against the actual code before prioritizing. Budget ~35% false-positive rate for AI reviews.\r\n81: \r\n82: ---\r\n83: \r\n84: ## Pre-Modification Rule (CRITICAL)\r\n85: \r\n86: > **Before changing ANY value, ALWAYS search first!**\r\n87: \r\n88: ```bash\r\n89: # Search for the value you're about to change\r\n90: grep -r \"value_to_change\" .\r\n91: ```\r\n92: \r\n93: This single habit prevents most \"forgot to update X\" bugs.\r\n94: \r\n95: ---\r\n96: \r\n97: ## How to Use This Directory\r\n98: \r\n99: 1. **Before coding**: Skim the relevant thinking guide\r\n100: 2. **During coding**: If something feels repetitive or complex, check the guides\r\n101: 3. **After bugs**: Add new insights to the relevant guide (learn from mistakes)\r\n102: \r\n103: ---\r\n104: \r\n105: ## Contributing\r\n106: \r\n107: Found a new \"didn't think of that\" moment? Add it to the relevant guide.\r\n108: \r\n109: ---\r\n110: \r\n111: **Core Principle**: 30 minutes of thinking saves 3 hours of debugging.\r\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}}
